===Glyph origin===
{{Han etyl}}
[[File:Evolution of Oracle Bone script for He-V2.png|thumb|right|420px|Evolution of the character 河]]
* '''Oracle bone script''':
** ''Shi group (thick strokes) / 師肥筆'': {{Han compound|水|何|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=water}}.
** ''Variant'': {{Han compound|水|可|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p}} (phonetic drawn wavy like water; possibly original form of {{zh-l|歌}}, representing wavy sound).
** ''Corruption'': Confused with {{zh-l|何}} ({{zh-l|水}} corrupted to {{zh-l|人}}; {{zh-l|可}} corrupted to a shoulder burden, possibly original form of {{zh-l|柯}}).
** ''He group / 何組'': {{Han compound|水|何|ls=psc}} (more pictographic).
** ''Huang group / 黃組'': Compound of {{zh-l|欠}} / {{zh-l|旡}}, a shoulder burden, and {{zh-l|水}}.
* '''Western Zhou bronze''': Inherits Huang group structure; adds {{zh-l|口}}.
* Possibly related to {{och-l|何|tr=*gˤaj}} (to bear).

===Etymology===
Originally particularly used for the [[Yellow River]], with other rivers known as {{och-l|水}}. Later extended generally as a word for rivers in northern China but the influence of {{och-l|江|[[Yangtze]] [[river|River]]; [[river]] in [[southern]] [[China]]}} has meant that modern usage tends to favor it for shorter rivers and creeks except in historical cases.

Further etymology uncertain. Various hypotheses have been proposed:
* {{der|zh|sit}}. Either:
** Cognate with {{cog|bo|རྒལ||to cross, to traverse}} ({{zh-ref|Coblin, 1986}}), or 
** Derived from {{och-l|湖|lake}} ({{zh-ref|Matisoff, 1995}}), or
** that the Yellow River's name stems from the shape, which resembles an adze handle {{och-l|柯}}, of a stretch spanning 500 to 600 kilometers "from {{w|Hejin|Héjīn}}, {{w|Tong Pass|Tóngguān}} to east of {{w|Zhengzhou|Zhèngzhōu}}" ({{zh-ref|Takashima, 2012}}).
* A Mongolic loan. Compare {{cog|xgn-pro|*gowl||river}} > {{cog|mn|ᠭᠣᠣᠯ||river}} ({{zh-ref|Norman and Mei, 1976}}). This was argued against in {{zh-ref|Zhang (1998)}}. 
** {{zh-ref|Miyake (2017)}} surmises that {{cog|xgn-pro|*gowl||river}} and {{och-l|河|tr= *[C.g]ˤaj < *[C.g]ˤal)}}, even if related, might have come from a third source language, e.g. {{w|Xiongnu language|Xiongnu}}. If so, compare also {{cog|qfa-yen-pro|*xur₁||water}} in light of Xiongnu's speculated linguistic affinity with the {{w|Yeniseian languages}}.

